module

public import Mathlib.Algebra.Module.Torsion.PrimaryComponent
public import Mathlib.NumberTheory.Padics.PadicIntegers
public import Mathlib.RingTheory.MvPowerSeries.Equiv
public import StacksCore.Tactic.StacksAttribute
public import StacksFrontier.RingTheory.LocalCohomology.ModelComparison.DerivedTorsion

/-!
# The inverse systems `H⁰_J(M/IⁿM)` need not be Mittag-Leffler

Let `A = ℤ_p[[x, y]]`, let `I = (p)` and `J = (p, x)`, and let `M = A/(xy - p)`. The point of
this file is the computation of the images of the inverse system `n ↦ H⁰_J(M/pⁿM)`: the image of
`H⁰_J(M/pⁿM) → H⁰_J(M/pM)` is the ideal generated by `yⁿ` in `M/pM = A/(p, xy)`. These images
strictly decrease and never stabilise, so the inverse system fails the Mittag-Leffler condition
that the proof of Stacks 0EFL would like to have.

The two inclusions are genuinely different. That `yⁿ` is in the image is the identity
`xⁿyⁿ = pⁿ` in `M`. That nothing else is, is the assertion that the `x`-power-torsion of
`A/(xy - p, pⁿ)` is carried by the ideal `(xy - p, yⁿ)`, which is proved here by expanding an
equation `x^k f = g(xy - p) + pⁿ h` in the `x`-adic coefficients: the coefficients `g_i` of `g`
satisfy `p g_i = y g_{i-1} + pⁿ h_i`, which forces `p^{n-1-j}` to divide the `y`-coefficient
`g_{i,j}` all the way along the chain, and the surviving coefficient of `f` therefore lies in
`(p, yⁿ)`.

## Main results

* `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.quotientEquiv` — the presentation
  `M/pⁿM = A/(xy - p, pⁿ)` used throughout, and
  `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.modIdeal_one_eq` — its case `M/pM = A/(p, xy)`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.mem_primaryComponent_iff` — an element of
  `M/pⁿM` lies in `H⁰_J(M/pⁿM)` exactly when some `x^k` kills it.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.mem_span_of_pow_mul_mem` — if `x^k f` lies in
  `(xy - p, pⁿ)` then `f` lies in `(p, xy, yⁿ)`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.imageSections_eq` — **Stacks 0EFM**: the image of
  `H⁰_J(M/pⁿM) → H⁰_J(M/pM)` is the ideal generated by `yⁿ` in `M/pM = A/(p, xy)`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.varY_pow_notMem` — `yⁿ` is not in `(p, xy, yⁿ⁺¹)`,
  so these images strictly decrease:
  `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.imageSections_lt`.
* `Stacks.Algebraization.MittagLefflerFailure.not_mittagLeffler` — consequently the inverse
  system `n ↦ H⁰_J(M/pⁿM)` does not satisfy the Mittag-Leffler condition.

## Implementation notes

The ring `ℤ_p[[x, y]]` is `MvPowerSeries (Fin 2) ℤ_[p]`, with `x = X 0` and `y = X 1`, so that
the two variables are on an equal footing as in the source; `p` is the natural-number cast.

Degree-zero local cohomology `H⁰_J(N)` is the `J`-power-torsion submodule
`Ideal.primaryComponent`, i.e. `{z | ∃ k, Jᵏ z = 0}`. This is the elementary description, and it
is the library's `H⁰` on modules: `sectionsWithSupportIn_eq_primaryComponent` records the
instance of `Stacks.LocalCohomology.sectionsWithSupportIn_zeroLocus_eq_primaryComponent`
identifying it with the sections supported in `V(J)`.

The `x`-adic coefficient computation is carried out in `ℤ_p[[y]][[x]]`, which is the same ring
written as an iterated power series ring, and transported along the algebra isomorphism
`MvPowerSeries.optionEquivLeft`; this is what makes the two levels of coefficients — in `x` and
then in `y` — available as `PowerSeries.coeff`.

Since `H⁰_J(M/pM)` injects into `M/pM`, equality of images of the transition maps inside
`H⁰_J(M/pM)` is equality of their images inside `M/pM`, and the images are computed there.

